WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:11.420
BOB KILLEN: Hello, everyone.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.860
And welcome to What's
Next in Kubernetes.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.220
I'm Bob Killen, a program
manager within Google's Open

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.120
Source Programs Office,
also a longtime contributor

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.920
to the Open Source
Kubernetes project.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.690
And I'm here to
talk a little bit

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:31.760
about some of the overarching
future paths for Kubernetes.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.320
But before I can dig
into what's next,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.740
I need to talk a little
about where we've been,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.440
what it's taken for
Kubernetes to become

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.755
the foundational platform
that powers so much today.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.370
From a high level, you can sort
of break down Kubernetes phases

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.450
into three broad layers that
highlight a focus or theme

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.050
in the project's lifecycle.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.380
The first and the
longest was four years,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.040
from June 2014 to March 2018.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.860
This is a period of
rapid growth, where

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.630
all the various
resource types are

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.570
being defined and solidified.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.030
These are the basic
components that

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.560
are used by everything else.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.110
Think of the core workloads,
such as deployments,

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.070
stateful sets, and
all the others.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:29.050
These core workloads graduated
to GA in the 1.9 release

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.110
and was shortly
thereafter recognized

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.960
by the Cloud Native Computing
Foundation's Technical

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.280
Oversight Committee, approving
the project for graduation.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.750
Kubernetes was the first
project to achieve the status.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.330
It was recognized
for being stable,

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.900
used by a number of companies,
having a diverse contributor

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.570
base and solid
project governance.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.450
This is the foundational stage.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:02.170
And every future thing
is built on top of it.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.890
The second phase, from March
2018 to September 2019,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.520
is marked by the graduation of
custom resource definitions,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:10.960
or CRDs--

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.350
a unified pattern to
extend Kubernetes.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.220
It created an entire ecosystem
allowing people, projects,

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.350
and organizations
to create and manage

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.580
their own applications in
any other sort of Kubernetes

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:26.780
resource.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.240
Instead of managing
these deployments,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.310
you can now directly
manage things,

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.260
like a database
or a game server,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.420
in a Kubernetes native way.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.270
It was huge for the
cloud native landscape.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.350
And if you look at the CNCF,
with its 100 or so projects,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.960
you'll find that the majority
of them are using CRDs.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.440
This last phase, from
late 2019 to August 2020,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.250
isn't noted by a new feature,
but a shift in the mentality

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.100
of the project.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.050
We built our core
workload types.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.690
We've made the project
easy to extend.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.750
And now with more users
than ever using the project,

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.300
we shifted to support
mobility and stability.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.090
Policies and processes
were put in place

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.970
to help ensure every
feature being developed

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.420
was observable, scalable,
could be rolled back,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.060
and a slew of other important
things for general operations.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.000
And in doing so, we
were more comfortable

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.720
expanding our support window,
with new releases being

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.100
supported for a full year.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.850
We built our core, we made it
easy to extend and integrate

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.070
with, and then
committed ourselves

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:45.740
to a stronger support policy.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.772
So that takes us to now
and where we're going.

00:03:48.772 --> 00:03:50.980
And I'll keep the theme for
this one sort of a secret

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.060
to the end.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.070
But I want to give
you an idea of some

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.740
of the features and areas
of where we're focusing.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.900
And for that, I picked
two big things to follow.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.000
The first is multi-cluster.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.540
So why multi-cluster?

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.020
Kubernetes has done a lot to
help people rethink and reshape

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.940
their architecture
and build more

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.470
resilient and scalable systems.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.380
But for many use
cases, you can't just

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.500
build a larger cluster.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.410
Say you want global availability
and to minimize the latency

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.820
to your customers.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.530
That's something
you can't easily

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.030
do with a single cluster.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:34.420
Or you might want to scale
and burst your workloads,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.140
especially in something like a
hybrid environment where you've

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.870
outgrown your on-prem hardware
and want to burst to the cloud.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:46.200
You may also be working with
privileged or sensitive data,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.080
and want to maximize your
security by using clusters

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.680
as a security boundary.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.710
Or lastly, you may just want
to make your application more

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.160
resilient by running multiple
clusters in HA and to segment

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.870
your failure domains.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:01.340
The list goes on.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.710
And it's often one
of the first places

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.080
organizations go to after
getting up and going

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.150
with a single cluster.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.150
Now with that, I'm going to
go out a little on a limb.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:19.410
But in a multi-cluster world,
clusters are the new pod.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.170
As management of Kubernetes
clusters becomes easier,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:25.980
it allows you to focus on
the important parts, namely

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.210
your application.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.170
Clusters are becoming a tightly
coupled grouping of these app

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.990
components that you might
want to scale and secure

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.690
independently.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.490
So just like a deployment
manages the pods and help scale

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.310
them up and down and
manages their life cycle,

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.300
you should be able to do
that with clusters too.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.235
So why has this been so hard?

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.910
Well, to be frank,
multi-cluster configurations

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.150
in their current form are
incredibly complex with a lot

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.840
of moving parts.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.910
If you have a
database in cluster A

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.370
that should be accessible to
some component in cluster B,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.560
it requires a lot of
additional plumbing

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.770
of services between clusters--

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.380
often, additional load
balancers or a mix of them

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.430
with a service mesh.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:19.460
It's not fun and
by no means easy.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.520
Also, to be frank,
Kubernetes was honestly

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.590
not really designed
for multi-cluster

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.290
in mind initially.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.690
But this has been
long recognized

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.250
by the upstream community.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.920
And there have been several
initiatives targeting

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.780
and improving this use case.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.100
The first is the introduction
of multi-cluster services,

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.100
a new CRD that standardizes
the way in which you

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.190
can share Kubernetes
services across clusters.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.370
No more additional provisioning
of load balancers, no more

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:58.090
hodgepodge custom DNS rules
that require manual updating.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:01.600
You define a cluster set or a
group of two or more clusters

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.390
and decide which
services in each cluster

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.770
you want to expose.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.480
And a new controller will
just take care of the rest.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.820
For your applications,
all you have to do

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.930
is them to a new cluster set
service DNS entry instead

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.990
of the regular cluster.local
one that we all

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:21.490
are sort of used to.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:24.620
And with that, your traffic
will be routed appropriately.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.290
Now, this is a
very simple model.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.890
And it was specifically
designed to be that way

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.980
to address the complexity
of cross cluster networking

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:37.990
and service discovery
to make it easy to use

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:42.060
as a drop in replacement
for current services.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.380
Now, there's a lot that
goes on under the hood.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:50.570
But you as a user do not need
to manage those aspects of it.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.100
So now that we have these
multi cluster services that

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:57.580
can help you manage your
cross cluster networking,

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.690
how about Ingress?

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.850
How do you manage
getting traffic

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.000
into your cluster
at a global level?

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.085
Well, there's something
for that too--

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.680
the Gateway API.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.430
Think of it as
Ingress version 2.0.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.650
It was designed with
all the lessons learned

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:19.390
from the original
Kubernetes Ingress

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:22.900
implementation with
many of the pain points,

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.880
such as excessive use of
annotations being addressed.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.400
And for the purposes
of multi-cluster,

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.700
it can use the exports from
the multi-cluster services

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.460
API as a valid
back end, allowing

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.624
you to manage your
Ingresses at a global level.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:46.060
Now, for GKE,
these two resources

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.420
have come together to offer
a very flexible and powerful

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.010
system.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.860
Within GKE, there are two
fundamental different types

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.720
of clusters--

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.840
standard, where you
retain full control,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.660
and autopilot, where much
of the management aspects

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:05.380
are abstracted away
and managed by Google.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:08.290
Autopilot is a great
option for many workloads.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:11.800
It comes with advantages like
better security and per pod

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:12.760
billing.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.630
But there are some workloads
that don't necessarily

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:23.390
lend itself to it well, namely
large stateful applications

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:27.740
or things where you may need to
run in a pod in privilege mode.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:30.640
But that's where
multi-cluster comes in.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.040
What if we can interconnect
and deploy apps

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.200
where they fit
best without having

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.550
to worry about the plumbing?

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.165
You'd be able to take advantage
of the greater security and per

00:09:41.165 --> 00:09:44.180
pod billing for most
resources in autopilot

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.540
and retain the greater
control over just the parts

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:51.760
of your stack that need it
in a standard cluster while,

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:55.568
honestly, managing them in
a dramatically easier way.

00:09:55.568 --> 00:09:57.735
And that's part of the
future we're working towards.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.300
So now that we've
covered multi-cluster,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:07.570
let's take a look at
the next focus area.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:11.960
And that's in the area of
AI/ML and batch computing.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.330
Now, this is an
area that's actually

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.010
near and dear to my heart.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:19.520
Before I started at Google,
I was heavily involved

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.620
in research computing
and exploring

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.560
Kubernetes for high
performance computing needs.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.110
And while there
have been advances

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.540
in what I'll say broadly
as research computing

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.180
on top of Kubernetes,
there has generally

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.480
been a much slower
rate of adoption of it

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.090
versus, I'll say, more
traditional applications.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:42.515
And that's because it's hard
for a number of reasons.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:48.200
First and foremost, the
Kubernetes scheduler

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:52.070
is built for bin packing,
or trying to cram as much

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.530
on a single node
as possible, which

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:58.950
tends to go against most
really resource intensive jobs.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:02.070
It also doesn't support the
concept of co-scheduling, where

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.780
you block scheduling
until there's

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.495
enough resources available
to run your entire job.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.910
The other thing is most of
these research applications also

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.530
require a lot of tuning--

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:17.760
the application, the underlying
system or host OS, and also

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.440
things like the API server.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.370
If you're spinning up hundreds
of thousands of tasks,

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.910
you need everything to be
working at peak performance

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:31.330
and to be able to handle
that sort of scale.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:35.200
Now, there are a few
things coming in the pipes

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.610
to help improve this.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.920
But I want to focus
on one thing that

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.710
has been an open request
for a very long time

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:45.115
and will help reduce the
complexity of work queues.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.360
Works queues are a
fairly simple in concept.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.230
They are a list of tasks or
data intended to be processed

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.120
by one or more workers.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.850
They are by far the
most common work pattern

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:02.430
seen in AI/ML and batch.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.370
You're providing a data
set and iterating over it

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:09.150
in parallel for
training or processing.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:13.230
And Kubernetes has not really
supported this workflow

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:13.890
natively.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.070
It's always required some
other external system,

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:21.413
such as Pub/Sub to keep track
of what's being processed.

00:12:21.413 --> 00:12:22.830
This also means
that you must have

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:26.490
bake in more sort of
middleware logic into your job,

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:27.930
just to be able to process it.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.460
It works.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:32.760
Organizations have been
using this pattern since jobs

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.890
were a thing in Kubernetes.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:36.720
But do you really
want to do that?

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.370
Or would you rather
rely on something

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:42.052
that could be done natively?

00:12:42.052 --> 00:12:42.885
I'd pick the latter.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:50.810
So in Kubernetes 1.21,
a new alpha feature

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.280
was added to jobs--

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.920
a completion mode, where if
you specify it as indexed,

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:58.500
each pod is given a
numbered identifier

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.130
with the index starting at 0.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.080
This index will be
used in the generation

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.050
of the pods hostnames, giving
it something predictable akin

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.700
to stateful sets.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.820
That index is also passed
into the pod as an environment

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:11.420
variable.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.260
And what this does is it
gives you the ability--

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:18.140
a built in native way to
statically assign data

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:22.190
to the processes of each pod.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:25.740
It will require a little bit
of planning ahead of time.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:28.030
But you won't have to
run an external system.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:31.950
You won't have to bake something
into your code for the job just

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:34.500
to pull items from the queue.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:37.150
It reduces the overall
complexity of work queues

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.140
significantly.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:42.550
And this seems
like a small thing.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:46.867
But it solves a problem
for a lot of people.

00:13:46.867 --> 00:13:49.200
In addition to this, there
are also several other things

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:52.440
in the works that
will enable or AI/ML

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.370
and batch types of workloads--

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.730
things like the
scheduler framework,

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:02.570
where instead of trying to
make the committee scheduler be

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:06.470
everything to everyone,
many more hooks and methods

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:09.030
of integrating with
it have been created.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.820
It's now significantly easier
to extend and customize

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:15.180
how scheduling is done
by assigning workloads

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.695
to different
scheduling profiles.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.420
Jobs can also now be suspended.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.960
And what this means is that
if you say a higher priority

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.450
job is to be scheduled,
the lower priority job

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:30.730
can be suspended until
the higher priority

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.900
job is completed.

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:34.300
It doesn't have
to be rescheduled.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:38.170
Work can pick back up when the
higher priority job is done--

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:41.620
something we've seen in
a lot of classic batch

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.930
systems for years.

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:47.530
Lastly, there's been some
changes to the Kubernetes API

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:51.650
server so it can be tuned
to support the exceedingly

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:55.700
high throughput of requests that
often come in with batch job

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.200
oriented systems.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.000
And all these
things combined will

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.710
make running AI/ML and batch
workloads significantly easier

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:08.110
on top of Kubernetes.

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:12.220
And at this point with
that sort of short preview

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:15.010
of the improvements to
things like multi-cluster

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.500
and AI/ML and
batch, you may have

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:20.110
started to pick up on a theme.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.000
What is Kubernetes?

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.800
Kubernetes is foundational.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:31.280
We spent four years
building a strong base.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.560
All the core workloads
and design patterns

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:36.166
that you depend on
today started here.

00:15:36.166 --> 00:15:38.100
It is extensible.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.950
A year and a half were spent
making it easy for people

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:44.820
to adopt, build, and
extend on that foundation,

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:50.750
creating an ecosystem that is
just absolutely massive today.

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.330
Kubernetes is mature.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:55.310
We put a lot of
effort into making

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:59.360
sure you can depend on every
feature and every release.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:04.430
And we demonstrated this by
extending our support window.

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:08.710
So what is Kubernetes now?

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:11.020
Is it easy?

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:14.210
Well, we're not there yet.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:17.320
We're making it
easier for you to use.

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:20.320
We're reducing much
of the complexity that

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.480
has forced people
into workarounds

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:27.040
and building our large,
more complex systems.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.140
A lot of these features
and improvements

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.750
may seem minor at first look.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.330
But all of them
are there to help

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.790
improve the various
workflows that people

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:39.550
have built around and
on top of Kubernetes.

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:43.360
And as we make Kubernetes
easier for people to use,

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.030
it will also enable a
whole new set of use cases

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:48.740
that we haven't imagined yet.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:52.650
So Kubernetes isn't easy yet.

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:53.640
But we'll get there.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.870
And with that, if you are
interested in learning more

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.400
about some of the stuff
I talked about today,

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.339
here are some
additional resources

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:05.690
that you might find useful.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:09.319
In particular, I want to
highlight the Learn Kubernetes

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:10.609
with Google Video series.

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:12.829
You'll find videos
going over how

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:15.890
you can get going with
multi-cluster index jobs

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.589
and quite a bit more.

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:19.138
Thank you.

00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:21.430
And I look forward to building
the future of Kubernetes

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.129
with you.

